// @PLN85 A.1 — borrowing-read aliasing (#426).  STILL RED — but the 2026-06-22
// re-attempt (after the store-reuse-after-free SUBSTRATE fix landed, commit
// 782937e9) found the residual blocker is NOT a substrate bug: it is a LANGUAGE
// DESIGN CONFLICT.  #426's premise — that `a = vv[0]` (A) and `c = o.inner.v`
// (C) "must COPY" — CONTRADICTS the documented vector-element VIEW semantics.
// Routed forward as a design decision, NOT a localized codegen fix.
//
//   A  vector INDEX read         `a = vv[0]`
//   B  fn-return-of-index        `b = idx0(w){ w[0] }`
//   C  nested struct-field read  `c = o.inner.v`
//
// WHY A/C CANNOT BE A COPY (the matrix-first finding, both backends):
//   1. DOCUMENTED VIEW SEMANTICS (#338, LOFT.md § Vectors / § Variables): "Reading
//      a struct ELEMENT into a local (`e = v[i]`) yields a dep-tracked VIEW of the
//      slot's record (`e.f = x` mutates `v[i]`)."  On base, `a = vv[0]` for a
//      `vector<vector<T>>` is a VIEW too — `vv[0] += [9]` makes `len(a) == 4`
//      CONSISTENTLY with the struct-element view.  The probe's "must COPY (observed
//      4: aliases)" treats the documented, deliberate VIEW as a bug — it is not.
//   2. #415 DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED index reads (OWNERSHIP_MODEL row 102 + the #415
//      regression test: "narrowed to STRUCT field reads — a vector INDEX read keeps
//      its existing nested-stride path").  #426 re-opens exactly that exclusion.
//   3. tests/scripts/294-vector-element-view-semantics.loft PINS element-read =
//      view (`test_element_read_is_view`: `e = v[0]; e.val = 99; assert v[0].val
//      == 99`).  A copy would break it (it passes today only because the LHS there
//      is a STRUCT element, not a vector, so the copy predicate would not fire —
//      but the SAME view rule applies to vector elements).
//   4. REAL-CONSUMER WRITE-THROUGH (p379, hex_world `set_cell`): `cells =
//      chunk.ck_cells; cells[i] = value` RELIES on the field read aliasing to
//      mutate through.  Copying it makes `self: &World` "never modified" — a
//      parse error / silent data loss.  Generalizing the #415 copy to the
//      nested-field base regressed p379 (verified).
//   The carried borrow dep CANNOT distinguish a read-only copy from a write-through
//   alias; that needs the `find_field_written_vars` mutation analysis (the
//   OWNERSHIP_MODEL borrow checker).  So A/C are a DESIGN reconciliation
//   (copy-vs-view for vector elements), not a dep-driven codegen patch.
//
// B (return path) is a SEPARATE store-lifetime SUBSTRATE bug, NOT design:
//   Routing `{ w[0] }` through `copy_borrow_tail_into_retbuf` (the #415/a2 helper)
//   DOES copy `w[0]` into `__retbuf`, but the helper's `OpFreeRef(__fwd)` then frees
//   a store the FREELIST recycles into the NEXT allocation, corrupting a subsequent
//   borrowing-read copy (`b = idx0(ww); c = by.v` → `len(c) == 0`).  getv's struct-
//   field tail does not hit it (its `__fwd` views the arg's base, not a nested
//   element store).  Repro banked: /tmp/p_followups/p426B_returnbuf_store_reuse.loft.
//   This is the a7-class return-buffer substrate — its own investigation.
//
// Workaround for A/C value-semantics (verified both backends): force an owned copy
// — `a = []; a += vv[0]`.  The substrate fix 782937e9 (3-deep corruption) DID land
// and IS unblocking — it is just not the blocker #426 needs.

struct Inner { v: vector<integer> }
struct Outer { inner: Inner }

fn idx0(w: vector<vector<integer>>) -> vector<integer> { w[0] }

fn main() {
  // A — vector INDEX read
  vv: vector<vector<integer>> = [[1, 2, 3]];
  a = vv[0];
  vv[0] += [9];
  assert(len(a) == 3, "A a=vv[0] must COPY (observed 4: aliases vv[0])");

  // B — fn returns a vector INDEX element
  ww: vector<vector<integer>> = [[1, 2, 3]];
  b = idx0(ww);
  ww[0] += [9];
  assert(len(b) == 3, "B b=idx0(ww) must COPY (observed 4)");

  // C — NESTED struct-field vector read
  o = Outer { inner: Inner { v: [1, 2, 3] } };
  c = o.inner.v;
  o.inner.v += [9];
  assert(len(c) == 3, "C c=o.inner.v must COPY (observed 4)");

  println("borrowing-read aliasing: all COPY (A.1 landed)");
}
